hls: a reader at the end of a live playlist keeps the playlist's pace

A reader that has read every segment a live playlist lists waits, and
reads the playlist again when a segment's length has passed since it
last read it: by then the playlist has moved on by one segment. Two
things made that reading late, each time anew.

The time was counted from when the last reading was done, with its
answer taken in: later than the moment the playlist was asked for by
what the answer took to come. And the reader looked whether the time
had come once every tenth of a second, so that it read the playlist up
to a tenth of a second after its time.

Each reading then counted from itself. Against a source that makes a
segment every 1.92 seconds, a reader on the same machine asked for the
playlist 3 to 108 ms later each time than a segment's length after the
time before, 30 to 60 ms on average; one whose playlist takes 0.3 s to
come, 0.32 to 0.42 s later each time. The reader fell behind the
playlist by that much with every segment, until it was a whole segment
behind, found two new ones in the list, and began falling behind again.

Whoever plays what the reader hands over got each segment that much
later than the one before. A player with seconds in hand saw its store
thin out and fill up again. One that had just stalled at the end of the
playlist plays on with the one segment that then came, and has the next
coming just as that one ends: it ran dry a second time some ten seconds
later, when the lateness had added up to more than it had to spare.

The reader now sleeps until the time has come and not past it, in
pieces of a tenth of a second at most, so that an interrupt is heard as
soon as it was. And when the reading it waited for finds that the
playlist has moved on, the next interval is counted from the time that
reading was due at, not from when it was done. The playlist is asked
for a segment's length after it was asked for, which is where RFC 8216
counts from (6.3.4), and neither the time an answer takes nor the
lateness of one waking is carried into the next.

Nothing else is changed. A reading that was not waited for (its time
had passed when the reader came to the end of a segment), one made a
whole interval late (a process that was held up), and one that finds
nothing new, which is made again half a target duration later, count
from when they were done, as before. The playlist is asked for no more
often than it was: once for each segment.

--- a/libavformat/hls.c
+++ b/libavformat/hls.c
@@ -1780,6 +1780,8 @@
 
     if (!v->input || (c->http_persistent && v->input_read_done)) {
         int64_t reload_interval;
+        /* When the list was to be read again, once that was waited for. */
+        int64_t due = AV_NOPTS_VALUE;
 
         /* Check that the playlist is still needed before opening a new
          * segment. */
@@ -1802,6 +1804,11 @@
         if (!v->finished &&
             av_gettime_relative() - v->last_load_time >= reload_interval) {
             int64_t listed_seq_no = v->listed_seq_no;
+            /* Whether this reading is one that was waited for and is made
+             * as its time comes: not a whole interval after it, as by a
+             * reader that was held up meanwhile. */
+            int in_time = due != AV_NOPTS_VALUE &&
+                          av_gettime_relative() - due < reload_interval;
             if ((ret = parse_playlist(c, v->url, v, NULL)) < 0) {
                 if (ret != AVERROR_EXIT)
                     av_log(v->parent, AV_LOG_WARNING, "Failed to reload playlist %d\n",
@@ -1812,6 +1819,16 @@
                 v->start_seq_no + v->n_segments < listed_seq_no &&
                 v->cur_seq_no >= v->start_seq_no + v->n_segments)
                 join_restarted_playlist(c, v);
+            /* A list that was waited for, and has moved on, is read again
+             * an interval after the time this reading was due at: the
+             * time at which the list was found to have its next segment,
+             * and at which it will have the one after. Counted from when
+             * this reading was done, which is later by what the reading
+             * took and by how late the reader woke for it, each reading
+             * would come that much later than the list moves on, and each
+             * segment that much later than the one before it. */
+            if (in_time && v->cur_seq_no < v->start_seq_no + v->n_segments)
+                v->last_load_time = due;
             /* If we need to reload the playlist again below (if
              * there's still no more segments), switch to a reload
              * interval of half the target duration. */
@@ -1850,12 +1867,17 @@
             av_log(v->parent, AV_LOG_WARNING, "The m3u8 list sequence may have been wrapped.\n");
         }
         if (v->cur_seq_no >= v->start_seq_no + v->n_segments) {
+            int64_t left;
             if (v->finished || v->is_subtitle)
                 return AVERROR_EOF;
-            while (av_gettime_relative() - v->last_load_time < reload_interval) {
+            /* Slept until the time comes, and not past it: a tenth of a
+             * second at a time at most, to hear of an interrupt between
+             * them as before. */
+            due = v->last_load_time + reload_interval;
+            while ((left = due - av_gettime_relative()) > 0) {
                 if (ff_check_interrupt(c->interrupt_callback))
                     return AVERROR_EXIT;
-                av_usleep(100*1000);
+                av_usleep(FFMIN(left, 100*1000));
             }
             /* Enough time has elapsed since the last reload */
             goto reload;
